Q. Can you explain how garbage collection and memory management happen internally?

Java manages memory automatically through the JVM, primarily using the heap and stack. Objects created using new are allocated on the heap, while local variables, method parameters, and method call information are stored in stack frames.

When an object is no longer reachable from any live reference, it becomes eligible for garbage collection. The JVM's Garbage Collector identifies such unreachable objects and reclaims their heap memory automatically.

Internally, modern JVMs generally use generational garbage collection. The heap is divided conceptually into young and old generations. Most newly created objects start in the young generation. Short-lived objects are usually collected during minor or young-generation collections, while objects that survive multiple collections may be promoted to the old generation.

Garbage collectors use techniques such as marking reachable objects, reclaiming or compacting memory, depending on the collector. The JVM can also perform compaction to reduce fragmentation.

The stack is managed differently: when a method finishes, its stack frame is removed automatically. Heap memory, however, is managed by the Garbage Collector.

Garbage collection is automatic, but it does not mean memory leaks are impossible. If an application accidentally keeps references to objects that it no longer needs, those objects remain reachable and cannot be collected.”





