Q7. Can you provide a hierarchy of memory areas, like Heap, Method Area, etc.?

The JVM divides its runtime memory into several major areas: Heap, Method Area, Java Stacks, PC Registers, and Native Method Stacks.

The Heap is shared among all threads and stores objects and arrays. 
The Method Area is also shared and stores class-level information such as 
class metadata, runtime constant pools, and method-related information. In HotSpot JVMs, the Method Area is implemented using Metaspace.

Each thread has its own Java Stack, which contains stack frames for method calls, 
including local variables, operand stacks, and references. Each thread also has its 
own Program Counter, or PC Register, which tracks the current JVM instruction being executed. 
Finally, the Native Method Stack supports execution of native methods, such as methods implemented through JNI.

So, at a high level, Heap and Method Area are shared memory areas, while the Java Stack, PC Register, and Native Method Stack are thread-private.




