7. Can you explain how Java handles memory management and garbage collection?


Java uses automatic memory management through the JVM, so developers generally don't have to manually allocate and deallocate memory.

At a high level, JVM memory is divided into areas such as the heap, method area, Java stacks, PC registers, and native method stacks. The heap is shared among threads and is where objects and arrays are generally allocated. Each thread has its own stack, which contains stack frames for method calls, local variables, and operand stacks.

Garbage collection primarily manages objects in the heap. When an object is no longer reachable from the JVM's GC roots, it becomes eligible for garbage collection. The garbage collector identifies such unreachable objects and reclaims their memory.

Modern JVMs commonly use generational garbage collection because most objects are short-lived. Newly created objects are generally handled in the young generation, and objects that survive multiple collections may be promoted to the old generation. Different garbage collectors use different strategies for marking, reclaiming, and sometimes compacting memory.

Java's automatic garbage collection reduces problems such as manual memory deallocation and dangling pointers, but it doesn't completely prevent memory leaks. A memory leak can still occur when an application unintentionally keeps references to objects that it no longer needs, making those objects reachable and therefore not collectible.

So, in summary, Java manages memory automatically using the JVM, while the Garbage Collector reclaims heap memory from objects that are no longer reachable.



